// Year 2 reading, part 3: more stories. Five more fables retold for this app, six new original
// adventures (a rainy Grand Prix, a satellite repair, a desert rally, a hero without powers, a
// knight and a dragon, a night at the dinosaur museum) and three more Beatrix Potter tales.
// Each passage ends with greater-depth items (ids ending .d1, .d2…): inference from evidence,
// prediction, the writer’s choices and links to other texts in the app.

import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';

const PASSAGES = JSON.parse(readFileSync(new URL('./passages3.json', import.meta.url), 'utf8'));
const P = (key) => ({ passage: PASSAGES[key].passage, passageSource: PASSAGES[key].passageSource });
const fact = (id, topic, prompt, answer, distractors, explain, extra = {}) => ({ id: `reading.s3.${id}`, topic: `reading.${topic}`, kind: 'fact', prompt, answer, distractors, explain, ...extra });
const mcq = (id, topic, prompt, options, explain, extra = {}) => ({ id: `reading.s3.${id}`, topic: `reading.${topic}`, kind: 'mcq', prompt, options: options.map(([text, correct, feedback]) => ({ text, correct: !!correct, feedback })), explain, ...extra });
const order = (id, topic, prompt, items, explain, extra = {}) => ({ id: `reading.s3.${id}`, topic: `reading.${topic}`, kind: 'order', prompt, items, labels: null, explain, ...extra });
const choiceOnly = { formats: ['choice'] };
const own = 'A story written for this app';
const fable = 'A fable by Aesop, retold for this app';

// --- fables, retold -------------------------------------------------------------------------
const CROW = { passage: 'The Crow and the Jug\n\nIt was the hottest day of the summer. A crow had flown a long way and she was very thirsty.\n\nAt last she saw a tall jug standing by a garden wall. There was a little water at the very bottom. The crow pushed her beak into the jug, but it was too narrow and the water was too low. She could not reach it.\n\nShe tried to tip the jug over, but it was too heavy.\n\nThe crow did not give up. She looked around and saw some small pebbles on the path. She had an idea.\n\nShe picked up one pebble and dropped it into the jug. Plop! Then another, and another. Each pebble pushed the water up a little higher.\n\nAfter fifty pebbles, the water was right at the top. The crow drank and drank until she was not thirsty any more.\n\nLittle by little does the trick.', passageSource: fable };
const MICE = { passage: 'The Town Mouse and the Country Mouse\n\nA country mouse lived in a field. One day her cousin from the town came to visit. The country mouse gave him barley, beans and a drink from the stream.\n\n“How can you eat such plain food?” said the town mouse. “Come to the town with me. I will show you how to live!”\n\nSo they went to the town. In a big house, on a long table, there were cakes, cheese and jelly left over from a party. The country mouse could not believe her eyes.\n\nBut just as she took her first bite, the door burst open. A cook came in with a broom! Then a cat sprang out from under a chair. The two mice ran for their lives and hid in a tiny hole, shaking.\n\nWhen it was quiet again, the country mouse picked up her bag.\n\n“Thank you, cousin,” she said. “But I would rather eat beans in peace than cake in fear.”\n\nAnd she went home to her quiet field.', passageSource: fable };
const DOG = { passage: 'The Dog and His Reflection\n\nA dog was trotting home with a big juicy bone in his mouth. He felt very pleased with himself.\n\nOn the way he had to cross a little bridge over a stream. As he crossed, he looked down into the water.\n\nThere he saw another dog, with another big bone! The dog did not know he was looking at his own reflection.\n\n“That bone looks even bigger than mine,” he thought. “I want it too.”\n\nSo he opened his mouth to grab the other bone and gave a loud bark. Splash! His own bone fell into the stream and sank to the bottom.\n\nThe other dog had vanished, and so had the bone. The dog walked home sadly with nothing at all.\n\nIf you are greedy, you can lose what you already have.', passageSource: fable };
const GOOSE = { passage: 'The Goose That Laid the Golden Eggs\n\nA farmer and his wife had a very special goose. Every morning she laid an egg made of shining gold.\n\nThe farmer sold the eggs at the market, and soon he and his wife were rich. They bought new clothes, a new cart and a fine horse.\n\nBut the farmer was not happy. “One golden egg a day is too slow,” he grumbled. “The goose must be full of gold inside. If we open her up, we can have all the gold at once!”\n\nHis wife agreed. So they did. But inside, the goose was just like any other goose. There was no gold at all.\n\nAnd now there would never be any more golden eggs.\n\nThe farmer and his wife sat down and cried. They had been too impatient, and they had lost everything.\n\nWanting too much, too quickly, can leave you with nothing.', passageSource: fable };
const OAK = { passage: 'The Oak and the Reed\n\nA tall oak tree stood beside a river. Down by the water grew a thin green reed.\n\n“Look at you,” said the oak. “You bend over whenever a little breeze blows. I am strong. I never bend for anyone.”\n\nThe reed said nothing.\n\nThat night a great storm came. The wind howled and roared. The reed bent low, right down to the water, and let the wind rush over it.\n\nThe oak stood straight and proud. It would not bend. The wind pushed and pushed. Then, with a terrible crack, the oak broke and crashed into the river.\n\nIn the morning the sun came out. The little reed stood up again, as green as ever.\n\nIt is sometimes wiser to bend than to break.', passageSource: fable };

// --- original adventures ----------------------------------------------------------------------
const RAIN = { passage: 'The Rain Race\n\nThe Grand Prix had just begun when the rain came. Big drops drummed on the track and the road turned shiny and slippery.\n\nBlaze was in the lead. Turbo was right behind him, trying hard to catch up.\n\nOn the sharpest corner, Turbo’s wheels lost their grip. He spun round and round and slid off the track into the gravel. His wheels whirred, but he was stuck.\n\nBlaze saw him in his mirror. He could keep going and win the race easily. But he remembered how Turbo had once been kind to him.\n\nBlaze slowed down, turned off the track and drove up behind Turbo. “Hold on!” he called. He pushed with his front bumper. Turbo’s wheels found the grass, then the road.\n\nThe other cars zoomed past them both. Turbo and Blaze crossed the line together, last of all, covered in mud.\n\nThe crowd cheered louder for them than for the winner.\n\n“We came last,” said Turbo.\n\n“No,” said Blaze, smiling. “We came together.”', passageSource: own };
const SAT = { passage: 'Bleep and the Broken Satellite\n\nHigh above the Earth, a weather satellite had stopped working. Without it, nobody could tell whether a storm was coming.\n\nCaptain Nova flew her spaceship up close to the satellite. She floated out in her spacesuit, with Bleep the robot tucked under her arm.\n\nThere was a small hatch on the side of the satellite. Captain Nova opened it and peered in. “The broken part is right at the back,” she said. “My hands are much too big to reach it.”\n\nBleep beeped bravely. He was small. He could fit.\n\nSlowly, carefully, Bleep squeezed through the hatch. Inside it was dark and full of wires. He switched on his headlight. There it was: a loose bolt, wobbling in its hole.\n\nBleep reached out his little tool arm and turned the bolt, tighter and tighter, until it would not wobble any more.\n\nThe satellite’s lights blinked on. Blue, green, blue.\n\nBack on Earth, the weather maps came to life again.\n\n“Being small,” said Captain Nova, “is exactly what we needed today.”', passageSource: own };
const SAND = { passage: 'The Desert Rally\n\nRosa’s team were racing across the desert. The sand was golden and the sky was bright blue.\n\nThen, far away, a brown cloud rose up. It grew bigger and bigger. “Sandstorm!” shouted Rosa.\n\nThe wind whipped up the sand until nobody could see more than a few steps. The racing car spluttered and stopped. Sand had blocked its air filter, and the engine could not breathe.\n\nRosa pulled on her goggles and tied a scarf over her nose and mouth. She tied a rope from the truck to her belt, so she could not get lost.\n\nThen she walked out into the storm, step by step, holding the rope.\n\nShe found the car, opened the bonnet and took out the filter. It was full of sand. She tapped it, shook it and blew on it until it was clean, then clicked it back in place.\n\nThe engine coughed, then roared.\n\nWhen the storm passed, the team cheered. They did not win the rally, but every single car in their team reached the finish.', passageSource: own };
const NOPOW = { passage: '{hero} and the Sniffly Day\n\nOne morning {hero} woke up with a terrible cold. Achoo! When the hero tried to fly, nothing happened. When the hero tried to lift the sofa, it would not budge. The superpowers had gone, just for the day.\n\nThat afternoon a little girl ran up, crying. “My kitten is stuck on the roof of the library!”\n\n{hero} looked up at the high roof. With no flying, how could anyone get up there?\n\nThe hero thought hard. Then the hero ran to the fire station and knocked on the door. “I need your tallest ladder,” said {hero}, “and some help to hold it.”\n\nThe firefighters carried the ladder over and held it tight. {hero} climbed up, step by step, legs shaking a little. It was a long way down.\n\nAt the top, the kitten was hiding behind a chimney. {hero} spoke softly until the kitten crept close, then tucked it inside a warm jacket and climbed down again.\n\nThe little girl hugged her kitten.\n\n“You don’t need superpowers to be brave,” said the fire chief. “You just did it without them.”', passageSource: own };
const DRAGON = { passage: 'The Knight and the Dragon\n\nEvery night a loud, sad roar echoed around Castle Rook. The people were frightened. “There is a dragon in the hills,” they said. “It will eat us all!”\n\nSir Rowan, the youngest knight, put on his armour and rode into the hills. He was scared, but he kept going.\n\nIn a dark cave he found the dragon. It was enormous and green, with smoke curling from its nose. Sir Rowan held up his shield.\n\nBut the dragon did not attack. It just sat there, holding out one huge foot and groaning.\n\nSir Rowan looked closer. A long, sharp thorn was stuck in the dragon’s toe!\n\nVery carefully, the knight took hold of the thorn and pulled. Out it came. The dragon sighed a long, warm sigh of relief.\n\nThe next morning, Sir Rowan rode back to the castle with the dragon flying above him. The people screamed, until they saw the dragon land gently and bow its head.\n\nFrom that day on, the dragon lived at Castle Rook. Every winter it lit all the fires in the great hall with one puff, and nobody was cold again.', passageSource: own };
const MUSEUM = { passage: 'A Night at the Dinosaur Museum\n\nMaya and Sam were having a sleepover at the dinosaur museum. Their sleeping bags were laid out right under the giant skeleton of a Diplodocus.\n\nWhen the lights went out, the museum was very quiet and very dark.\n\nThen they heard it. A low rumbling, growling noise. GRRRRRR.\n\n“A dinosaur!” whispered Sam, pulling his sleeping bag over his head.\n\nMaya was scared too, but she wanted to find out. She switched on her torch and followed the sound, past the T. rex teeth and the fossil eggs, round the corner and into the next hall.\n\nThere stood Mr Patel the cleaner, pushing a big floor-polishing machine. GRRRRRR, it went, as it spun round and round.\n\nMr Patel laughed. “Did I wake you? Come and see something special.”\n\nHe shone his torch on a glass case. Inside was a real fossil footprint, as big as Maya’s sleeping bag.\n\n“That was made by a dinosaur that walked here millions of years ago,” said Mr Patel.\n\nMaya ran to fetch Sam. Being brave, she decided, was much more fun than hiding.', passageSource: own };

